    "FIX-1 / CODEX FINDING 1 [medium] — `sed|gsed|perl|ruby -i` parsed the edit PROGRAM as a path. WHAT CHANGED: the branch previously pushed EVERY non-flag token through consider(), so the script/expression argument — which is prose, not a path — was tested as a write target and an allowed write was denied for merely mentioning a protected segment. Replaced with an explicit operand rule, written into the header comment directly above the code it describes so the two cannot drift: (1) a token starting with `-` is a flag, never a target; (2) a short cluster containing `e`/`E`/`f` supplies the program — GLUED when characters follow that letter (`-e'EXPR'`, `-pe'EXPR'`), otherwise the NEXT non-flag token is the program (`-e EXPR`, `-pe EXPR`, `-f script.sed`) — and either way the program is consumed, never considered; (3) `--expression`/`--file`, with or without `=`, do the same; (4) if no flag supplied a program, the FIRST bare operand IS the program (`sed -i 's/a/b/' FILE...`) and is skipped; (5) every remaining bare operand is a file and IS considered. In-place detection was also moved so it walks FLAG tokens only — previously a program argument could itself be mistaken for `-i`. ARMS THAT NOW COVER IT: 44, 45, 46 (the review's three named cases, all ALLOW), 47, 48, 49, 50 (the four that were actually RED), with 51/51b/51c/51d/51e/52 as the paired true-positives proving the false-positive fix did not open a false negative.",
